Why this license, briefly:

- Standard MIT grants the permissions above (use, copy, modify, merge,
  publish, distribute, sublicense, sell) with the copyright and permission
  notices preserved.
- The attribution clause (clause 2) is a standard, common addition to MIT
  for solo portfolio projects. It requires downstream users to keep the
  author's name and contact URLs visible in their distribution. This is
  not a restriction on commercial use; it is a requirement that the
  authorship be acknowledged. The LinkedIn URL is included deliberately: it
  is the identity link that proves who the author is.
- There is no content carve-out here, unlike the stackclimb.com source
  license. This repository IS tooling — a skill is meant to be installed and
  run. The documents a skill produces belong to whoever ran it.
- Stackclimb is named in the attribution because every project sits under
  that umbrella.

Closes issue #23. Before this file existed, the repository was public with no
license at all, which under default copyright means all rights reserved — the
opposite of what a suite of installable, reusable skills is for.
